@@ -98,7 +98,35 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); AuthZ stays out of scope per #20 and HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (exactly-once), and only for a caller allowed to call on its queue (see [Queue Management and Call Access](#queue-management-and-call-access)). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+
+### Queue Management and Call Access
+
+Owners and admins manage the queues of their entity. Employees only call tickets, and only on queues they were granted.
+
+**Queue lifecycle (owner and admin only):**
+
+- **Create:** a name (trimmed, 1–255 characters) and an optional slug. The slug defaults to the slugified name (`Atendimento Geral` → `atendimento-geral`) and is slugified either way. New queues are active.
+- **Slug uniqueness:** unique **within an entity**; the same slug may exist in another entity. A duplicate is rejected as a validation error.
+- **Update:** name, slug and active flag can change in any combination. Keeping the queue's own slug is fine; taking another queue's slug is rejected.
+- **Retire, never delete:** a queue is retired by deactivation (`is_active = false`). Its tickets are preserved, and issuing or calling on it fails with `InactiveQueueException` until it is reactivated. There is no delete action for queues.
+
+**Per-queue grants (`queue_access`):**
+
+- Owners and admins set the permissions an employee membership holds on a queue. The only permission today is `call`. A non-empty list grants it; an empty list revokes it.
+- Owners and admins never need a grant: their role already allows calling on every queue of the entity.
+- Employees cannot grant access, including to themselves.
+- Every grant and revoke is audited (`queue.access.granted` and `queue.access.revoked`, with the acting user, queue and membership). A revoke with no existing grant is not audited.
+
+**Who may call tickets of a queue:**
+
+| Who | May call on the queue |
+| --- | --- |
+| Owner or admin of the queue's entity | Yes, always |
+| Employee with a `queue_access` grant on that queue whose permissions include `call` | Yes |
+| Employee without such a grant, member of another entity, non-member | No |
+
+A denied call changes nothing. Both call paths enforce the rule: `CallNextTicket` (every call mode) and `Ticket::call()`. The checks run in this order: queue is active (`InactiveQueueException`), caller is allowed (`AuthorizationException`), ticket is still waiting (`LogicException` for `Ticket::call()`).
 
